1
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:10,750
In this video we're going to learn some
basic barista science about milk,

2
00:00:10,750 --> 00:00:14,860
how to heat and texture milk to form a
delicious base for drinks,

3
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:23,199
and how to pour it to make drinks look pretty. 
Before we start steaming and pouring milk,

4
00:00:23,199 --> 00:00:28,390
it is important to learn some basic
milk chemistry and the rules that govern the way we treat milk when we steam it.

5
00:00:28,390 --> 00:00:35,800
As far as we're concerned as baristas, milk consists of three main components:
sugars, proteins, and fats.

6
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:41,379
Let's discuss each one and the role it plays in both preparation and taste.

7
00:00:41,379 --> 00:00:46,239
Let's start with sugars. The main sugar in milk is lactose and is actually very sweet.

8
00:00:46,239 --> 00:00:50,559
This explains by well heated and
textured cappuccinos and lattes often

9
00:00:50,559 --> 00:00:55,899
don't need extra sugar to taste sweet.
This plays a role in the temperature of

10
00:00:55,899 --> 00:00:59,739
our beverages because we want to taste
every bit of sweetness we can pull out of the milk.

11
00:00:59,739 --> 00:01:14,710
Imagine a fresh piping hot piece of pizza. If you put it in your mouth immediately,  all you taste is heat and pain.

12
00:01:14,710 --> 00:01:18,070
If you let that same pizza cool a little, though you can taste everything that's delicious and wonderful about

13
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:23,020
melted cheese and saucy bread. By the
same logic then you don't want your

14
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:30,119
lattes to be too hot to taste all that
lovely lactose next up our proteins.

15
00:01:30,119 --> 00:01:34,960
Proteins begin as coiled balls in cold
milk and through the addition of heat

16
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:40,210
uncoil to expose a hydrophilic (or
water-loving) end and hydrophobic (or water hating) end.

17
00:01:40,210 --> 00:01:46,030
When we pull air into the milk with the steam wand the

18
00:01:46,030 --> 00:01:49,840
hydrophobic ends of the proteins will
hide from the water in the milk inside

19
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:54,340
the air bubble causing the hydrophilic
ends to form a barrier around the bubble

20
00:01:54,340 --> 00:01:59,869
keeping its shape. This is known as a
surfactant.

21
00:01:59,869 --> 00:02:03,950
The surfactant qualities of proteins are
what holds the air bubbles together to

22
00:02:03,950 --> 00:02:07,969
create milk foam and will keep its shape
until about a 160°F (or 71°C),

23
00:02:07,969 --> 00:02:13,849
at which point the proteins will fall
apart or denature.  This causes the air

24
00:02:13,849 --> 00:02:19,220
bubbles the proteins are holding to pop
and the milk foam to deteriorate-- yet

25
00:02:19,220 --> 00:02:24,799
another reason we don't want to steam
too hot. Our third and final milk

26
00:02:24,799 --> 00:02:30,560
component is fat. Fats affect the
thickness of the milk and quality and

27
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:36,170
longevity of the foam. The more fat
present in the milk the thicker the

28
00:02:36,170 --> 00:02:42,349
liquid and the longer the foam will last.
Imagine milk foam as a series of bubbles

29
00:02:42,349 --> 00:02:47,239
stacked on top of one another. In between
each one of these bubbles is a tiny

30
00:02:47,239 --> 00:02:53,000
space that will let liquid fall through
it. The more fat present in the milk the

31
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,900
thicker that liquid will be and the
longer it will take to fall between the gaps

32
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:02,650
and the bubbles, creating a stiff
dry layer of foam on top of liquid milk.

33
00:03:02,650 --> 00:03:07,040
The other effect fats have on the
steaming process is that it gives the

34
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:12,680
hydrophobic parts of proteins another
option to hide inside. The less fat

35
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:17,299
present in milk, the more the proteins
will focus on the air we inject, creating

36
00:03:17,299 --> 00:03:21,980
larger amounts of foam. Now that we've
talked about the three main components

37
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:26,450
in milk, let's discuss four key
temperatures and how they affect each

38
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:34,130
component. Our first temperature is 40°F (4°C) or right out of the fridge.

39
00:03:34,130 --> 00:03:39,049
At this point the sugars are too cold to
really taste, the proteins are in coiled balls,

40
00:03:39,049 --> 00:03:46,459
and the fats are in solid globules.
Let's add some heat and see what happens.

41
00:03:46,459 --> 00:03:53,010
Next up is a 100°F (37°C) or about
people temperature. Here the sugars are

42
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:57,450
just starting to get yummy, the proteins
are uncoiling, and the fats are melting

43
00:03:57,450 --> 00:04:03,900
into oils. Because the fats are going
from a solid to a liquid, it gives the

44
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:07,880
proteins another option of something to
grab on to, disrupting foam formation.

45
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:12,180
It's for this reason that we want to
stop adding air when the milk reaches a

46
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:17,700
100°F (37°C), when it's just starting
to feel warm. At 140°F (60°C),

47
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:22,620
the sugars are in full effect,
the proteins are uncoiled, and all the

48
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:28,260
fats have turned to oil. This is our
ideal tasting temperature, so stop the

49
00:04:28,260 --> 00:04:33,690
steaming process once the milk is 140°F (60°C) or too hot to touch.

50
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:38,700
At 160°F (71°C), the sugars are too hot
to taste, the proteins are denaturing, and

51
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:45,930
the fats... well I guess they're doing just
fine. At this point, not only is the milk

52
00:04:45,930 --> 00:04:50,760
not sweet and the foam starting to fail,
but the taste of denatured protein is

53
00:04:50,760 --> 00:04:55,860
bitter and eggy--a poor complement to
espresso. Now that we have a basic

54
00:04:55,860 --> 00:05:00,680
understanding of the rules behind milk
steaming, let's talk about the process.

55
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:04,919
The first step is steaming perfectly
textured milk for an espresso beverage

56
00:05:04,919 --> 00:05:09,630
is to determine the right amount of cold
milk to pour into the appropriately

57
00:05:09,630 --> 00:05:15,390
sized pitcher for that drink. The final
volume of milk in the pitcher after

58
00:05:15,390 --> 00:05:20,010
steaming should equal the volume of the
drink you're about to pour, and with

59
00:05:20,010 --> 00:05:23,729
about an ounce (30mL) of espresso already taking up room in the cup, that should

60
00:05:23,729 --> 00:05:26,640
leave you with an ounce of milk (30mL) left
over in the pitcher when you're through pouring.

61
00:05:26,640 --> 00:05:34,850
For lattes we want 25%
aeration and for cappuccinos, 50%.

62
00:05:34,850 --> 00:05:38,940
Therefore the amount of cold milk you
pour into the pitcher at the start

63
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:43,320
should be such that after aeration
you've attained the volume of the drink

64
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:48,630
you're about to pour. Now let's go
through each base espresso drink from

65
00:05:48,630 --> 00:05:52,349
smallest to largest and talk about how
much cold milk to pour into which

66
00:05:52,349 --> 00:06:03,060
pitcher for that drink.
Let's start with cappuccinos and cortados to stay.

67
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:09,090
In most steaming pitchers the bottom of the spout indicates the halfway point for the total volume of the pitcher. Therefore for this six ounce (178 mL) drink,

68
00:06:09,090 --> 00:06:14,009
pour in 3 ounces (88 mL) of cold milk
to reach halfway to the bottom of the

69
00:06:14,009 --> 00:06:19,770
spout. After aeration the foam should
reach the bottom of the spout, totaling

70
00:06:19,770 --> 00:06:27,030
six ounces (178 mL) of aerated milk at 50%, just
enough to fill the cup. For eight ounce (236 mL) cappuccinos

71
00:06:27,030 --> 00:06:32,159
to go, pour in four ounces (118 mL) of
cold milk to reach just below the bottom

72
00:06:32,159 --> 00:06:37,099
of the spout. After aeration the milk
should reach halfway up the spout,

73
00:06:37,099 --> 00:06:44,940
totaling eight ounces (178 mL) of aerated milk at
50%. For an 8 ounce (236 mL) latte to go pour 6 ounces (178 mL)

74
00:06:44,940 --> 00:06:48,960
of cold milk into a 12 ounce (355 mL)
steaming pitcher to reach the bottom of

75
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:55,469
the spout.  After aeration, the milk should
reach halfway up the spout for 8 ounces (236 mL)

76
00:06:55,469 --> 00:07:03,870
of total steamed milk aerated at 25%.
For a 12 ounce (355 mL) latte, pour 9 ounces (266mL) of

77
00:07:03,870 --> 00:07:07,979
cold milk into a 20 ounce (590 mL) steaming
pitcher, to reach just below the bottom

78
00:07:07,979 --> 00:07:13,169
of the spout. After aeration the milk
should reach .25 of the way up the

79
00:07:13,169 --> 00:07:22,380
spout, totaling 12 ounces (355 mL) of steamed milk
at 25% aeration. For a 16-ounce (473 mL) latte use

80
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:28,289
a 20 ounce (590 mL) steaming pitcher and pour in
12 ounces (355 mL) of cold milk to reach just

81
00:07:28,289 --> 00:07:33,990
above the bottom of the spout. After
aeration the milk should reach .75 of

82
00:07:33,990 --> 00:07:40,310
the way up the spout for 16 ounces (473 mL) of
steamed milk aerated at 25%.

83
00:07:40,310 --> 00:07:44,759
Because steaming pitchers taper up from
the bottom, it will appear as though you

84
00:07:44,759 --> 00:07:51,930
have more foam in this size than others.
Last but not least, for a 20 ounce (590 mL) latte

85
00:07:51,930 --> 00:07:58,349
use a 36 ounce (~1L) steaming pitcher and pour
in 16 ounces (473 mL) of cold milk to reach just

86
00:07:58,349 --> 00:08:03,270
below the bottom of the spout.
After aeration the milk should reach a

87
00:08:03,270 --> 00:08:06,990
.25 of the way up the spout,
totaling 20 ounces (590 mL) of steamed milk

88
00:08:06,990 --> 00:08:13,379
aerated at 25%. Now that we know
the percentages of aeration for lattes

89
00:08:13,379 --> 00:08:17,759
and cappuccinos and which pitchers to
use for which drinks, let's talk about

90
00:08:17,759 --> 00:08:21,479
the mechanics of using the espresso
machine steam wand to create perfectly

91
00:08:21,479 --> 00:08:26,580
textured milk. We'll do these
demonstrations with milk enough for a 12

92
00:08:26,580 --> 00:08:31,560
ounce (355 mL) latte. We'll begin by using a 20
ounce (590 mL)  steaming pitcher and filling it

93
00:08:31,560 --> 00:08:37,110
with 9 ounces (266 mL) of cold milk to reach
just below the bottom of the spout.

94
00:08:37,110 --> 00:08:41,459
At the machine begin by opening the steam
valve for at least one second to remove

95
00:08:41,459 --> 00:08:45,209
any condensation from inside the steam
wand and prevent injecting your milk

96
00:08:45,209 --> 00:08:51,630
with water next position the steam wand
at a 10 to 20 degree angle straight out

97
00:08:51,630 --> 00:08:55,470
from the machine
this will position all the holes on the

98
00:08:55,470 --> 00:08:59,399
steam wand tip at a downward angle,
allowing it to pull air into the milk

99
00:08:59,399 --> 00:09:05,010
instead of skipping it across the
surface. Next submerge the steam wand

100
00:09:05,010 --> 00:09:08,910
into the milk just to the point where
the steam wand tip screws into the steam

101
00:09:08,910 --> 00:09:14,220
wand itself. This will allow you to open
the steam valve all the way without

102
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:19,769
immediately pulling in air. Tilt the
pitcher slightly to one side or the

103
00:09:19,769 --> 00:09:23,130
other allowing the angle of the steam
wand to push the milk in a circular

104
00:09:23,130 --> 00:09:28,290
motion around the pitcher. This allows
you to control the high water mark in

105
00:09:28,290 --> 00:09:34,769
the pitcher and inject air only when you
choose to. In one motion open the steam

106
00:09:34,769 --> 00:09:38,519
valve all the way and lower the pitcher
until you hear the steam wand

107
00:09:38,519 --> 00:09:42,110
start to pull air into the milk.

108
00:09:49,360 --> 00:09:55,160
The proper amount of aeration should
sound like constant paper tearing. If it

109
00:09:55,160 --> 00:09:58,670
sounds like an intermittent chirp it
means you're not adding enough air and

110
00:09:58,670 --> 00:10:03,530
should lower the pitcher more. If it
sounds like TV static, you're adding too

111
00:10:03,530 --> 00:10:09,080
much air and should raise the pitcher
slightly. Continue to consistently add

112
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:13,760
air until the side of the pitcher feels
warm to the touch or you have the

113
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:17,470
appropriate amount of aeration, whichever
comes first.

114
00:10:17,470 --> 00:10:22,550
To stop aerating, raise the pitcher just
enough to resubmerge the holes in the tip

115
00:10:22,550 --> 00:10:27,650
of the steam wand. Hold this position
allowing the steam wand to heat and

116
00:10:27,650 --> 00:10:34,190
swirl the milk in the pitcher until it
is 140°F (60°C) or too hot to touch.

117
00:10:34,190 --> 00:10:39,020
Once the milk is hot enough turn off the
steam valve in one motion lower the

118
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:43,130
pitcher to the counter, purge out any
residual milk on the inside of the steam

119
00:10:43,130 --> 00:10:48,530
wand, and thoroughly clean the outside of
the steam wand with a damp cloth.

120
00:10:48,530 --> 00:10:53,440
Now let's see the entire process live from
two different angles.

121
00:11:20,589 --> 00:11:24,860
Even if everything goes perfectly there
might be some small blemishes on the

122
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:29,089
surface of the milk when you're through.
So tap the pitcher on a hard surface to

123
00:11:29,089 --> 00:11:33,649
pop any visible bubbles and swirl the
milk for at least three seconds until

124
00:11:33,649 --> 00:11:40,100
it's glossy and shiny like wet paint or
marshmallow fluff. So far we've seen what

125
00:11:40,100 --> 00:11:44,329
happens when things go well so now let's
see what happens when we add not enough

126
00:11:44,329 --> 00:11:49,970
and too much air. If you don't add enough
air before the milk reaches a 100°F (37°C),

127
00:11:49,970 --> 00:11:53,720
there won't be enough foam
present in the milk to buffer the sound

128
00:11:53,720 --> 00:11:58,750
of aeration causing it to screech in
pain as you steam it.

129
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:10,460
The end result is aerated milk with a
thin watery texture and bubbles that

130
00:12:10,460 --> 00:12:15,560
won't pop out when you tap the pitcher.
Another result of not aerating enough is

131
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:20,660
that the milk won't expand. This means
you won't have enough steamed milk in

132
00:12:20,660 --> 00:12:26,090
the pitcher to fill the customers cup. If
you add too much air the amount of foam

133
00:12:26,090 --> 00:12:29,540
you're creating will buffer the sound of
steaming entirely making the whole

134
00:12:29,540 --> 00:12:32,530
process whisper-quiet.

135
00:12:41,130 --> 00:12:46,540
The result is thick pillowy foam that
stiffens quickly and is difficult to

136
00:12:46,540 --> 00:12:59,740
pour. So far we've talked about some
basic milk chemistry and the rules that

137
00:12:59,740 --> 00:13:04,930
govern the way we treat milk when we
steam it. We've also discussed good

138
00:13:04,930 --> 00:13:08,650
techniques to steam appropriate amounts
of milk into a delicious well textured

139
00:13:08,650 --> 00:13:15,460
base for lattes and cappuccinos. So now,
without further ado, let's finally get to

140
00:13:15,460 --> 00:13:21,820
the reason you've skipped ahead to this
part of the video: Latte art. A good way

141
00:13:21,820 --> 00:13:25,870
to think about latte art is like plating
for a chef. You've taken your time to

142
00:13:25,870 --> 00:13:29,500
craft a delicious shot of espresso, and
you've played by all the rules we've

143
00:13:29,500 --> 00:13:33,250
talked about so far to create a
perfectly textured milk base that will

144
00:13:33,250 --> 00:13:38,140
taste great with it. Latte art is your
chance to grab the customer's attention

145
00:13:38,140 --> 00:13:43,450
and communicate to them that this drink
is going to be amazing. The first

146
00:13:43,450 --> 00:13:47,440
variable we'll talk about is height or
the distance of the spout of the pitcher

147
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:53,680
from the surface of the liquid in the
cup. Imagine a bubble of milk foam as a

148
00:13:53,680 --> 00:14:00,370
white ball we're dropping into a colored
liquid. If the ball is submerged in the

149
00:14:00,370 --> 00:14:04,140
liquid it will be stained that color
when it pops back up to the surface,

150
00:14:04,140 --> 00:14:09,210
whereas if the ball remains on the
surface of a liquid, it will stay white.

151
00:14:09,210 --> 00:14:12,910
Dropping the ball from higher-up
guarantees that it will submerge when it

152
00:14:12,910 --> 00:14:21,190
hits the liquid, whereas dropping it from
lower will keep it on the surface.

153
00:14:21,190 --> 00:14:29,020
The goal of any latte art design is a crisp
white design on a dark brown surface.

154
00:14:29,020 --> 00:14:33,040
To accomplish this contrast we want to be
mindful of three main heights that we

155
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:38,680
can control while pouring. The first is
three inches (7.6 cm) above the surface of the

156
00:14:38,680 --> 00:14:43,000
liquid in the cup. This will cause the
milk foam to plunge below the surface of

157
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:48,930
the espresso keeping the surface of the
liquid in the cup dark brown.

158
00:14:48,960 --> 00:14:54,190
The second important height is about one
inch (2.5 cm) above the liquid in the cup.

159
00:14:54,190 --> 00:14:58,090
This will cause some of the foam to submerge
and some to float resulting in an

160
00:14:58,090 --> 00:15:05,410
undesirable hazy beige color. The way to
make sure your design is crisp white is

161
00:15:05,410 --> 00:15:09,160
to keep the spout of the pitcher within
a quarter inch (.6 cm) of the liquid in the cup

162
00:15:09,160 --> 00:15:14,560
when pouring the design. Just like in the
ball analogy, pouring with the spout of

163
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:18,190
the pitcher this close to the liquid in
the cup prevents the foam from

164
00:15:18,190 --> 00:15:23,680
submerging below the espresso and allows
it to retain its vibrant white color. In

165
00:15:23,680 --> 00:15:27,790
order to get as close as possible as
soon as possible tilt the cup at a

166
00:15:27,790 --> 00:15:31,600
thirty to forty-five degree angle and
make sure the pitcher is touching the

167
00:15:31,600 --> 00:15:35,350
lip of the cup the entire time you're
forming the design. Watch what happens

168
00:15:35,350 --> 00:15:38,440
when the cup is kept flat and the
pitcher rests on the rim of the cup.

169
00:15:38,440 --> 00:15:44,650
First nothing appears then beige then
crisp white as the liquid in the cup

170
00:15:44,650 --> 00:15:49,630
approaches the spout of the pitcher
now here's what to do to control this

171
00:15:49,630 --> 00:15:55,990
process begin by tilting the cup about
45 degrees and start pouring from a

172
00:15:55,990 --> 00:16:00,460
height of above three inches (7.6 cm) to fill the
cup without having any design show up on

173
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:06,339
the surface. As soon as the liquid
reaches the rim of the cup stop pouring

174
00:16:06,339 --> 00:16:11,880
to avoid the beige area entirely and
touch the pitcher to the rim of the cup.

175
00:16:11,880 --> 00:16:16,420
Quickly resume pouring with the pitcher
touching the cup. To pour a crisp white

176
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:23,850
design be sure to slowly untilt the
cup to keep from spilling as it fills.

177
00:16:23,850 --> 00:16:28,360
A good rule of thumb here is that if any
beige appears in your design that means

178
00:16:28,360 --> 00:16:31,950
the spout of the pitcher was not within
a quarter inch (.6 cm) of the liquid in the cup,

179
00:16:31,950 --> 00:16:36,100
meaning that our that the cup was not
tilted enough to let the pitcher reach

180
00:16:36,100 --> 00:16:40,839
the liquid or the pitcher was not
touching the rim of the cup. The next

181
00:16:40,839 --> 00:16:45,040
variable we had the tackle is changing
the flow rate from the pitcher and how

182
00:16:45,040 --> 00:16:49,750
that corresponds to building surface
tension in the cup. As we pour milk and

183
00:16:49,750 --> 00:16:54,610
foam into the cup surface tension begins
to build, starting with very low surface

184
00:16:54,610 --> 00:17:00,220
tension when the cup is empty and high
surface tension once the cup is full.

185
00:17:00,220 --> 00:17:07,079
As a result the sooner you begin design the
more the design can flow across the cup.

186
00:17:07,079 --> 00:17:13,959
The later you begin design the smaller
and more compressed it will be. To pour

187
00:17:13,959 --> 00:17:19,839
large designs that fill the cup entirely
tilt the cup aggressively and bring the

188
00:17:19,839 --> 00:17:24,290
pitcher down to the rim of the cup as
soon as the liquid reaches the rim.

189
00:17:24,290 --> 00:17:28,790
Because you've begun the design when the
surface tension is relatively low, it

190
00:17:28,790 --> 00:17:32,510
will allow it to flow across the cup
before the surface tension is high

191
00:17:32,510 --> 00:17:37,160
enough to compress it. Now that we've
talked about the timing aspect of

192
00:17:37,160 --> 00:17:42,950
surface tension, let's discuss the flow
rate aspect.  Because surface tension is

193
00:17:42,950 --> 00:17:47,210
building as we fill the cup, it's
important to continue to increase the

194
00:17:47,210 --> 00:17:53,270
rate of pour from the pitcher to keep
designs flowing. Begin designs with a

195
00:17:53,270 --> 00:17:58,430
relatively low flow rate and continue to
pour faster as the design progresses to

196
00:17:58,430 --> 00:18:02,300
keep the design flowing across the cup
and prevent foam from piling up at the

197
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:07,340
spout of the pitcher. Here is an example
of starting the design portion of a pour

198
00:18:07,340 --> 00:18:11,690
as soon as possible after the fill
portion. Note how it affects the design

199
00:18:11,690 --> 00:18:15,820
on the surface making it nice and big.

200
00:18:18,760 --> 00:18:23,450
Now watch what happens when you fill for
longer and start the design later in the

201
00:18:23,450 --> 00:18:28,370
pour. The design on the surface looks
small and compressed--like it never got a

202
00:18:28,370 --> 00:18:31,610
chance to flow across the cup because we
began the design when the surface

203
00:18:31,610 --> 00:18:38,750
tension was high. In each pour note how
the flow rate from the pitcher increases

204
00:18:38,750 --> 00:18:42,610
steadily as a design progresses.

205
00:18:49,619 --> 00:18:53,830
Now that we've talked about height and
the surface tension flow rate connection

206
00:18:53,830 --> 00:18:57,759
the final core variable we have to
understand is the position of the

207
00:18:57,759 --> 00:19:02,799
pitcher in the cup. To understand where
to place the pitcher, let's first talk

208
00:19:02,799 --> 00:19:07,210
about how milk flows from the pitcher
and around the cup. Similar to blowing up

209
00:19:07,210 --> 00:19:11,649
a balloon the design is going to form in
front of the pitcher traveling down

210
00:19:11,649 --> 00:19:17,440
towards the bottom of the cup and then
circling back up and around. With that in

211
00:19:17,440 --> 00:19:21,009
mind
imagine a crosshairs in the cup. The way

212
00:19:21,009 --> 00:19:25,299
to pour a centered design is to place the
spout of the pitcher slightly above the

213
00:19:25,299 --> 00:19:32,229
center, allowing the balloon to fill up
below the spout. If the spout is placed

214
00:19:32,229 --> 00:19:36,039
in the center of the cup, the balloon
will fill toward the bottom leaving the

215
00:19:36,039 --> 00:19:39,820
top half empty. If the spout of the
pitcher is placed towards the top of the

216
00:19:39,820 --> 00:19:44,649
cup the foam will run towards the bottom
but won't circle back around, creating a

217
00:19:44,649 --> 00:19:49,330
long shape instead of a nice circle. If
the spout is placed on either side of

218
00:19:49,330 --> 00:19:53,080
the cup, it will create a current
carrying the design around and making

219
00:19:53,080 --> 00:19:57,519
one side larger than the other. First
let's see what happens when you place

220
00:19:57,519 --> 00:20:00,789
the spout of the pitcher too low in the
cup right in the center of the

221
00:20:00,789 --> 00:20:04,840
crosshairs.
Note that the design fills at the bottom

222
00:20:04,840 --> 00:20:08,460
of the cup leaving the top empty.

223
00:20:15,170 --> 00:20:20,060
Next let's see what happens when the
spout is placed too high in the cup.

224
00:20:20,060 --> 00:20:24,270
Note that the design runs out of energy
before it reaches the bottom of the cup

225
00:20:24,270 --> 00:20:34,380
and is long and weird-looking instead of
nice and round. Now let's see what

226
00:20:34,380 --> 00:20:38,310
happens when the pitcher is placed on
one side of the cup or the other.

227
00:20:38,310 --> 00:20:42,450
Note the current that forms in a spiral
around the cup carrying the design with

228
00:20:42,450 --> 00:20:52,650
it and making one side larger than the
other. To help with consistency while

229
00:20:52,650 --> 00:20:56,430
you're learning, when you find a good
position take note of where the spout of

230
00:20:56,430 --> 00:21:02,100
the pitcher rests on the rim of the cup. For the purpose of this design and other

231
00:21:02,100 --> 00:21:06,270
round designs once the pitcher touches
the cup, maintain that position

232
00:21:06,270 --> 00:21:11,550
throughout the design. Letting the
pitcher drag backwards across the cup as

233
00:21:11,550 --> 00:21:16,200
you untilt will cause a stacked
triangular shape instead of keeping it

234
00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:20,700
nice and round. So far in these examples
we've been pouring a traditional

235
00:21:20,700 --> 00:21:24,420
cappuccino design referred to as a
monk's head. While a monk's head is

236
00:21:24,420 --> 00:21:28,350
perfectly fine in its own right, let's
put a tail at the bottom to transform it

237
00:21:28,350 --> 00:21:32,910
into a solid heart shape. To accomplish
this, start by forming a good solid

238
00:21:32,910 --> 00:21:37,560
monk's head by following the steps we've
talked about so far. When the cup is

239
00:21:37,560 --> 00:21:41,400
almost full, raise the pitcher back to
the three inch (7.6 cm) mark to stop actively

240
00:21:41,400 --> 00:21:45,660
forming a design and slowly move the
stream of milk to the bottom of the cup,

241
00:21:45,660 --> 00:21:50,160
drawing the monk's head to a point
forming the tail of the heart. When you

242
00:21:50,160 --> 00:21:55,330
reach the bottom of the cup abruptly
stop pouring to finish the design.

243
00:21:55,330 --> 00:21:59,470
This technique is referred to as the
draw through, and will finish most

244
00:21:59,470 --> 00:22:05,800
common latte art designs. When performing
any draw through make sure to cross back

245
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:11,800
into the three inch mark or you'll drag
a line of foam through the design. Also

246
00:22:11,800 --> 00:22:15,670
be careful to decrease your flow rate
and move slowly through the center of

247
00:22:15,670 --> 00:22:19,630
the design to prevent from sinking the
design below the surface or dragging it

248
00:22:19,630 --> 00:22:23,590
to the bottom of the cup. At this point
in the process we've learned to pour a

249
00:22:23,590 --> 00:22:30,610
clearly recognizable sought-after a piece
of latte art: the solid heart. Congrats! It

250
00:22:30,610 --> 00:22:35,080
is strongly advisable that you stop here
and perfect this design before moving on.

251
00:22:35,080 --> 00:22:39,430
A good barista should have at least one
design they can pour reliably for

252
00:22:39,430 --> 00:22:43,270
customers instead of pouring poorly
executed designs for them while they

253
00:22:43,270 --> 00:22:47,170
practice. Now that we can consistently
pour a solid heart let's give it some

254
00:22:47,170 --> 00:22:52,570
pizzazz and learn how to add texture or
ripples to a design. To add even layers

255
00:22:52,570 --> 00:22:57,460
to a design, maintain a consistent flow
rate from the pitcher and add a very

256
00:22:57,460 --> 00:23:02,980
slight back-and-forth motion with your
wrist. This will cause the milk to start

257
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:09,850
to swing in a pendulum motion inside the
pitcher. Note how even and consistent the

258
00:23:09,850 --> 00:23:14,440
rocking motion is of the milk inside the
pitcher and how little the pitcher is

259
00:23:14,440 --> 00:23:19,150
actually moving to accomplish this.
Because you're spreading the stream of

260
00:23:19,150 --> 00:23:24,820
milk out over a wider area the actual
force of the milk is lessened so it's

261
00:23:24,820 --> 00:23:28,420
important to keep your flow rate high
and not lose momentum as you pour the

262
00:23:28,420 --> 00:23:34,060
design. The goal is to have a consistent
even pendulum motion from the milk in

263
00:23:34,060 --> 00:23:38,410
the pitcher and avoid twitchiness by
keeping your flow rate consistent and

264
00:23:38,410 --> 00:23:44,620
your motions even. Here we'll use a
consistent pendulum motion and even flow

265
00:23:44,620 --> 00:23:48,880
rate to pour rippled heart. Do everything
we've done previously to pour a great

266
00:23:48,880 --> 00:23:54,210
solid heart, but just add the pendulum
motion from the pitcher.

267
00:24:03,529 --> 00:24:07,639
Here's what the same design looks like
when you're twitchy and inconsistent.

268
00:24:07,639 --> 00:24:11,669
Honestly this isn't the worst heart
we've ever seen but it's definitely not

269
00:24:11,669 --> 00:24:15,749
the consistent even rippled heart we poured
before, and this motion would be

270
00:24:15,749 --> 00:24:20,580
disastrous for other designs, as we'll
see next. Now that we're comfortable

271
00:24:20,580 --> 00:24:24,149
pouring different types of hearts, let's
use those skills to pour a more complex

272
00:24:24,149 --> 00:24:29,309
design. This next one's called a tulip
because it resembles a cute cartoonish

273
00:24:29,309 --> 00:24:34,529
flower coming out of the ground. This is
a great design for beginners because it

274
00:24:34,529 --> 00:24:37,409
gives you the chance to start and stop
to correct any mistakes you've made

275
00:24:37,409 --> 00:24:42,779
throughout the pour. Begin by pouring a
rippled heart shape in the center of the

276
00:24:42,779 --> 00:24:47,460
cup just like we've been doing. This time
though, instead of drawing through the

277
00:24:47,460 --> 00:24:53,119
bottom, stop pouring abruptly when the
top of the heart begins to take shape.

278
00:24:53,119 --> 00:24:57,749
Next, place the spout of the pitcher just
above where the heart shape meets and

279
00:24:57,749 --> 00:25:03,600
pour a solid heart shape into the
rippled base you just formed. If the cup

280
00:25:03,600 --> 00:25:07,230
is full at this point simply lift the
pitcher up and draw through into the

281
00:25:07,230 --> 00:25:12,240
base to finish the design. In this
example we still have some room in the

282
00:25:12,240 --> 00:25:16,169
cup so we're going to add another layer
just like we did before by placing the

283
00:25:16,169 --> 00:25:21,299
spout of the pitcher just above where
the base meets. After forming a solid

284
00:25:21,299 --> 00:25:24,419
heart shape at the top of the design,
we're going to finish it off by

285
00:25:24,419 --> 00:25:29,429
performing a draw through. The tulip
gives you a lot of options but as you

286
00:25:29,429 --> 00:25:33,149
can stop and start as many times as you
want, adding as many layers as you have

287
00:25:33,149 --> 00:25:38,249
room for. Also if you notice that your
design is getting off-center, you can

288
00:25:38,249 --> 00:25:42,240
correct that to a certain extent by
placing your layers at different points

289
00:25:42,240 --> 00:25:45,649
in the cup.
An important thing to keep in mind in

290
00:25:45,649 --> 00:25:48,889
this design is that the milk in the cup
and in the pitcher is going to start to

291
00:25:48,889 --> 00:25:53,149
separate if it sits for any appreciable
amount of time, so be sure to pause for

292
00:25:53,149 --> 00:25:56,779
as little time as possible in between
layers so the foam remains fluid and

293
00:25:56,779 --> 00:26:02,479
pourable. In latte art there are two main
shapes we can form, and how we combine

294
00:26:02,479 --> 00:26:08,679
those shapes determines the design. Those
shapes are heart shapes and leaf shapes.

295
00:26:08,679 --> 00:26:13,039
Heart shapes are formed by maintaining
your position in the cup as you pour or

296
00:26:13,039 --> 00:26:17,659
pushing forward slightly, resulting in
the round shape we've been pouring so

297
00:26:17,659 --> 00:26:22,070
far in this video. Leaf shapes on the
other hand are formed by pulling the

298
00:26:22,070 --> 00:26:26,570
pitcher backwards as you pour causing
the lines to stack up instead of curling

299
00:26:26,570 --> 00:26:29,840
around.
Let's employ this new technique to form

300
00:26:29,840 --> 00:26:33,710
a design called a rosetta.
The rosetta is a much more difficult

301
00:26:33,710 --> 00:26:37,519
design to pour because it involves a lot
of movement and doesn't give you much

302
00:26:37,519 --> 00:26:43,820
time to assess and adjust mid pour. Don't
be frustrated if this design takes a

303
00:26:43,820 --> 00:26:48,169
while to get down and remember not to
practice on customers. A great-looking

304
00:26:48,169 --> 00:26:53,450
solid heart always looks better than a
rickety rosetta. We're going to start the

305
00:26:53,450 --> 00:26:57,379
rosetta just like we started the rippled
heart and the tulip, by placing the

306
00:26:57,379 --> 00:27:00,619
spouts of the pitcher just above the
center of the liquid in the cup and

307
00:27:00,619 --> 00:27:05,419
rocking the pitcher side-to-side gently
to form a consistent even pendulum motion.

308
00:27:05,419 --> 00:27:09,769
Just like in the tulip we're
watching for the top of the heart shape

309
00:27:09,769 --> 00:27:15,139
to begin to form before we make a change.
This time though instead of stopping the

310
00:27:15,139 --> 00:27:19,399
pour abruptly we're going to slowly move
the pitcher backwards towards the top of

311
00:27:19,399 --> 00:27:24,549
the cup while keeping our flow rate
consistent and our pendulum motion even.

312
00:27:24,549 --> 00:27:29,359
Make sure not to lessen your flow rate
or make any jerky motions with the

313
00:27:29,359 --> 00:27:35,229
pitcher at this point as that will
result in twitchy looking uneven leaves.

314
00:27:36,800 --> 00:27:40,820
When the spout of the pitcher reaches
the top of the cup, hold that position

315
00:27:40,820 --> 00:27:43,990
until the cup is full to form a solid
heart shape.

316
00:27:43,990 --> 00:27:48,740
Once the cup is full, gently perform a
draw through to knit the leaves together

317
00:27:48,740 --> 00:27:55,340
pouring through the center to finish the
design. Now we've covered all the basic

318
00:27:55,340 --> 00:27:59,870
techniques and shapes to pour great
latte art. Once you're comfortable with

319
00:27:59,870 --> 00:28:03,650
everything we've covered so far, feel
free to get creative and combine these

320
00:28:03,650 --> 00:28:05,650
designs and techniques in different ways.

321
00:28:13,030 --> 00:28:19,610
Remember though that with great latte
art comes great responsibility, and when

322
00:28:19,610 --> 00:28:22,610
you pour something gorgeous for a
customer you're promising them you've

323
00:28:22,610 --> 00:28:26,630
done all the work up until that point to
make sure their drink is going to taste

324
00:28:26,630 --> 00:28:45,590
just as good as it looks.

